//? Title: Bresenham Lines: Integer Decisions and Pixel-Centre Ties
//? Subtitle: Actual all-octant errors, inclusive endpoints and safe widened arithmetic
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An integer raster line selects one pixel centre per position along its major
axis. Bresenham's method makes those choices with an incremental integer error.
A two-step segment first exposes the half-pixel tie convention. The original
line from (0,0) to (14,6) then supplies actual errors, selected centres and
geometric residuals. Signed-octant controls and exact output counts distinguish
the constant working state from the returned vector's storage. Ordinary points
and diagnostic records share one canonical native C++ recurrence.
)

\toc

> A half-pixel tie: which centre is selected

For the segment (0,0)→(2,1), the ideal height at x=1 is one half. Both centres
(1,0) and (1,1) are equally close. This library's directed, inclusive comparison
rule selects (1,1): at a tie the minor coordinate moves towards the destination.
The diagram uses equal coordinate scales; grey is the ideal segment, and the
blue path joins the actual returned centres. Those joins are a visual guide.
The hollow point is an equally close centre that this call did not select.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bresenham_midpoint.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bresenham_line_trace(0,0,2,1);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(200,1000);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke grid;grid.color={226,232,240};grid.width=1;
  for (int x=0;x<=2;++x) diagram.path({{double(x),-.4},{double(x),1.4}},grid);
  for (int y=0;y<=1;++y) diagram.path({{-.4,double(y)},{2.4,double(y)}},grid);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke ideal;ideal.color={148,163,184};ideal.width=3;ideal.dashed=true;
  diagram.path({{0,0},{2,1}},ideal);
  std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> centres;
  for (const auto& [x,y]:trace.points) centres.push_back({double(x),double(y)});
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke raster;raster.color={37,99,235};raster.width=3;
  diagram.path(centres,raster);
  for (const auto& point:centres) diagram.ellipse(point,.045,.045,{37,99,235},raster);
  diagram.ellipse({1,0},.045,.045,{255,255,255},raster)
      .text({0,-.17},"(0,0)").text({1,1.18},"chosen (1,1)").text({2,1.18},"(2,1)")
      .text({1,-.2},"other tie (1,0)").text({1.42,.48},"ideal y = 1/2")
      .text({1,-.65},std::format("e = {}; 2e = {}; step x = {}, step y = {}",
          trace.steps[0].error,trace.steps[0].doubled_error,trace.steps[0].step_x,trace.steps[0].step_y))
      .save_svg("build/bresenham_midpoint.svg");
  std::print("Forward centres: {}.\n\nReverse centres: {}.\n",trace.points,
      dm::algorithms::bresenham_line(2,1,0,0));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Forward centres: [(0, 0), (1, 1), (2, 1)].

Reverse centres: [(2, 1), (1, 0), (0, 0)].
\image(figures/bresenham_midpoint.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Reversing the segment selects (1,0) at the tie. Reversing the returned vector is therefore not always the same as rasterising the reversed endpoints.)
\alttext(A dashed segment from zero-zero to two-one crosses halfway between the candidate centres one-zero and one-one. The blue raster chooses one-one and then two-one. The unchosen one-zero centre is hollow.)

> The recurrence and a nearest-centre interpretation

Let dx=abs(x1−x0), dy=abs(y1−y0), and let sx and sy point towards the final
endpoint. Initialise the signed error to e=dx−dy. At each nonterminal centre,
save b=2e and apply both tests to that saved value:

$$
\begin{aligned}
b\ge-dy&:\quad x\leftarrow x+sx,\qquad e\leftarrow e-dy,\\
b\le dx&:\quad y\leftarrow y+sy,\qquad e\leftarrow e+dx.
\end{aligned}
$$

Both tests may fire, producing a diagonal move. The second test uses b from
before either move, even if the first move changed e. At the final endpoint,
record the centre and stop without moving. The horizontal or vertical case
is handled by the same loop, and coincident endpoints produce one centre.
Doubling is integer multiplication by the constant two; no slope division,
floating-point rounding or general multiplication is needed in the decision loop.

For a shallow line in coordinates directed from the start, write k for the
horizontal step count and let r_k be its chosen minor-coordinate offset.
The exact nearest-centre rule with this tie convention is

$$
r_k=\left\lfloor\frac{k\,dy}{dx}+\frac12\right\rfloor,
\qquad 0\le k\le dx.
$$

Steep lines exchange the major and minor axes; sign changes translate the
relative offsets back to the input coordinates. To derive the midpoint test,
compare the ideal next height (k+1)dy/dx with r_k+1/2. Multiplying by 2dx
eliminates division. Increasing k adds 2dy to that decision quantity; choosing
the next minor centre subtracts 2dx. The recurrence carries this information
with additions and integer comparisons. The nearest-centre formula implies
an absolute minor-coordinate residual at most one half; a selected centre
need not lie exactly on the continuous segment.

> Original example: actual centres and saved errors

The original endpoints are (0,0) and (14,6). The output contains fifteen
centres, including both endpoints. The table comes from the real native loop,
with outgoing flags shown for each current centre; terminal=true means that
no move is performed. The last row's doubled error is recorded for inspection
but is not used to choose another point.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bresenham_line.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bresenham_line_trace(0,0,14,6);
  std::print("Centres ({}): {}.\n\n",trace.points.size(),trace.points);
  std::print("| centre | error e | saved 2e | step x | step y | terminal |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  std::vector<double> x,y;
  for (const auto& row:trace.steps) {
    x.push_back(row.point.first);y.push_back(row.point.second);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",row.point,row.error,row.doubled_error,row.step_x,row.step_y,row.terminal);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().lines({0,14},{0,6},"ideal segment",dm::RGB{148,163,184},2)
      .points(x,y,"chosen centres",dm::RGB{37,99,235},5)
      .title("Original line: (0,0) to (14,6)").x_label("integer x coordinate").y_label("integer y coordinate")
      .y_limits(-.5,8).size(900,480).save_svg("build/bresenham_line.svg");
}
```
