\image(figures/bresenham_line.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original fifteen centres are preserved. The integer error is an algorithm state, not the plotted vertical distance. The blue points approximate the grey segment; they do not all sit exactly on it.)
\alttext(Fifteen blue pixel centres form a shallow staircase near the grey straight segment from zero-zero to fourteen-six. The endpoints lie exactly on the segment and interior centres alternate above and below it.)

After a horizontal offset h and vertical offset v, the recorded state obeys

$$
e=dx-dy-h\,dy+v\,dx.
$$

Every x update subtracts dy and every y update adds dx, so this invariant
follows immediately from the initial state. It also explains why e is not
itself a geometric distance: it combines the extents with both offsets.
The native tests verify this invariant for every record in their small and
random rational-oracle comparisons.

> All octants, directions and endpoint controls

Each signed pair below starts at the origin. Swapping the axes exercises
steep lines; changing the signs covers the other octants. The original steep
(3,10) and diagonal (7,7) controls are retained, followed by axis-aligned and
coincident endpoints. Each trace counts actual x, y and joint moves. There is
one joint move for every minor-axis update, so the diagonal count equals the
smaller extent. Counts include no outgoing move from the final endpoint.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::print("| endpoint | centres | x updates | y updates | diagonal updates |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [x,y]:std::vector<dm::algorithms::GridPoint>{{6,2},{2,6},{-2,6},{-6,2},{-6,-2},{-2,-6},{2,-6},{6,-2},
        {3,10},{7,7},{5,0},{0,-5},{0,0}}) {
    const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bresenham_line_trace(0,0,x,y);
    if (trace.points.size()!=dm::algorithms::bresenham_point_count(0,0,x,y)) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",dm::algorithms::GridPoint{x,y},trace.points.size(),
        trace.x_moves,trace.y_moves,trace.diagonal_moves);
  }
  const int lo=std::numeric_limits<int>::min(),hi=std::numeric_limits<int>::max();
  std::print("\nInclusive count across the full int x range: {}.\n",
      dm::algorithms::bresenham_point_count(lo,0,hi,0));
  try {
    dm::algorithms::bresenham_line_trace(lo,0,hi,0,100);
    return 1;
  } catch (const std::length_error&) {
    std::print("The 100-point limit rejects that complete raster before allocation.\n");
  }
}
```
