// result_end
\caption(The original line's maximum residual is 3/7 of a pixel; the directed tie reaches one half. The work table counts native records, with one terminal record excluded from outgoing decisions.)
\alttext(Blue residuals oscillate between minus three-sevenths and plus three-sevenths of a pixel. The short red tie example reaches the upper half-pixel bound. Grey lines mark plus and minus one half.)

With M=max(dx,dy), every major-axis coordinate advances exactly once per
nonterminal iteration, so the algorithm emits M+1 centres and makes M outgoing
decisions. Work is O(M+1). Its working arithmetic state is O(1), while the
returned vector uses O(M+1) storage. The trace adds a record of constant size
per centre. The optional positive trace point limit checks the complete count
before allocation; zero means no caller limit. Allocation failures propagate,
and no partial segment is returned. The input is deterministic and requires
no observational dataset or random seed.

> Reference and verification

J. E. Bresenham (1965), *Algorithm for computer control of a digital plotter*,
IBM Systems Journal 4(1), 25–30,
[doi:10.1147/sj.41.0025](https://doi.org/10.1147/sj.41.0025),
[original paper](https://janmr.com/files/papers/bresenham65.pdf).
The source develops incremental integer decisions and octant transforms;
this report inspects the library's compact symmetric all-octant recurrence
with its existing directed tie rule.

Native tests compare all 14,641 endpoint pairs in the square [-5,5]² with an
independent exact-rational nearest-centre formula. They also check signed
extremes on short segments, full-range counts and preflight limits, reversed
ties, axis exchange, the original centres, saved-error invariants and 1,000
random moderate segments. All eight focused cases pass the undefined-behaviour
sanitizer. Every report block is an editable complete C++ programme; figures
are local SVG files, not text dumped into the results.
